### Diagnostics

On the {{ device }}'s device page, select the three dots menu > **Download
diagnostics**. Look it over before posting it publicly.

### Reporting a problem

1. On the integration's page, select the three dots menu > **Enable debug logging**.
2. Make the problem happen again, and note the time.
3. Download diagnostics from the device page, and note the {{ device }}'s model.
4. Turn off debug logging; Home Assistant then offers the log as a download.
5. Look over both files, then open an issue in the
   [issue tracker](https://github.com/jasonaward/{{ repo }}/issues) with them, the
   time, and what you expected to see.

The [reporting guide](CONTRIBUTING.md#reporting) has more, including what to send for a
new or untested model. Report security issues privately, as described in
[SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md).

## Removing the integration

1. Go to **Settings** > **Devices & services** > **{{ name }}**, select the three
   dots menu, then **Delete**.
2. If you installed it with the Marketplace or HACS, remove it there too; otherwise
   delete `config/custom_components/{{ domain }}`.
3. Restart Home Assistant.

## Development

All of the code in this integration is written by AI, as part of learning when and how
AI-written code can be applied well, and of evaluating it as the technology improves.
The maintainer directs the work and makes the design decisions. Changes go through a
lengthy, iterative process of testing, verification, and validation, with real devices
where applicable and with accounts and services the maintainer uses. Before each
release, an independent AI-assisted audit checks it against Home Assistant's
integration quality scale and the maintainer's
[integration standard](https://github.com/jasonaward/ha-integration-standards),
alongside automated security scanning.

It's maintained as well as possible while the maintainer uses these devices and
services, and updated as needed to keep it working; if that changes, this README will
say so. Contributions are welcome: bug reports, ideas, and pull requests in the
[issue tracker](https://github.com/jasonaward/{{ repo }}/issues), and security
concerns through [private reporting](SECURITY.md). All of the code is open for anyone
to evaluate.

This describes the maintainer's aims and process, not a guarantee; see
[License and disclaimer](#license-and-disclaimer).

### Running the checks

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install pytest-homeassistant-custom-component -r requirements_test.txt ruff mypy
pytest -q --cov=custom_components --cov-report=json
python scripts/check_coverage.py coverage.json 95
ruff check . && ruff format --check .
mypy
```

